<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Jaffa (1192)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Jaffa of 1192 was the agreement that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>, concluded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> remained under Ayyubid control, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> kept a coastal strip from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and Christian pilgrims were allowed to visit the holy places. Tolerance of the Crusader coastal ports also served Ayyubid commercial interests.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ayyubid rulers tolerated Crusader enclaves on the coast, an arrangement formalised in successive truces from the <b>Treaty of Jaffa (1192)</b> onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Third Crusade recovered Acre in 1191 and much of the coast, and the <b>Treaty of Jaffa</b> in 1192 confirmed a reduced kingdom with Acre as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard I of England fought at Jaffa in 1192, and the <b>Treaty of Jaffa</b> ended the Third Crusade there.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-jaffa-and-ascalon">County of Jaffa and Ascalon</a></li></ul></section>
